Samdech Techo Hun Sen Calls for 8 Points to Continue to Participate in Promoting Literacy in Cambodia More Effectively
(Phnom Penh): On the National Literacy Day on September 8, 2021, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, called on eight points for national, international, community and all citizens to continue to participate in promoting literacy in Cambodia more effectively.
Through a congratulatory message on the celebration of the National Literacy Day on September 8, Samdech Techo Hun Sen called for the following:
* First, the relevant ministries and institutions must develop policies, legal frameworks, letters, norms and mechanisms necessary to lead, manage and implement effectively and quality to ensure lifelong learning for all citizens and in line with the new development objectives. Sustainable as well as socio-economic recovery after the Kovid-19 crisis.
* Second, all ministries, institutions, educational institutions, study centers, and pagodas must continue to develop lifelong educational programs in both literacy, vocational, and information technology to meet the needs of students.
* Third: Local authorities at all levels must pay more attention to support the development of learning center infrastructure and encourage people who do not study or drop out of school to have the opportunity to learn. In particular, we must strive to make it easier for people with disabilities to have access to equitable and inclusive education.
* Fourth: All media, both public and private, please help to widely disseminate all literacy programs so that our people have a broader understanding under the theme "Literacy is the foundation of lifelong learning."
* Fifth: You are students in public and private educational institutions, Youth Union of Youth Federation of Cambodia, Youth Scouts and Youth of the Cambodian Red Cross must actively participate in volunteering to teach illiterate people in your community. Compatriots who are parents or guardians also want to encourage their children to learn consistently and get good results for everyone.
* Sixth: Monasteries and temples must be joined by the movement of monks, clergy, and nuns to promote professional literacy programs by educating them about the importance of moral education and promoting a culture of citizenship. Global.
Seventh, development partners and non-governmental organizations must increase cooperation and strengthen partnerships with government institutions to support both resources and techniques for all educational programs, especially literacy programs linked to vocational education to run smoothly. Regularly.
* And 8th: The President of the factory, enterprise and private company, please encourage the opening of literacy, combined with other professional training for workers for the benefit of them and for the benefit of factory owners, all of us. We must remember that "being literate, thinking and making the right decisions, being literate leads to the development of a more prosperous community", being literate, harmonizing the lives of individuals, families and society.
