The NEC announces the submission and receipt of applications for registration of national and international observers and agents of political parties for the 2021 voter list review and registration (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): National Organizing Committee The Election (NEC) has issued a notification on the submission and receipt of applications for registration of national and international observers and agents of political parties for the 2021 voter list and voter registration review.
The announcement received by Fresh News this morning, September 7, 2021, stated that it has informed the associations, national and international NGOs and all political parties as follows:
* The deadline for submitting and receiving applications for registration as a National Observer is from September 12, 2021 to February 2, 2022.
* Associations or non-governmental organizations wishing to participate in the observation must collect the sample application form for registration of observers at the NEC office and must submit their application for registration of observers at the NEC office every working hour. Morning from 7:00 to 11:30 and afternoon from 14:00 to 17:30 (Office of Political Parties and Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations, 9th floor).
* National observers who have been officially recognized and authorized by the NEC and have received the National Observer ID card from the Capital / Provincial Election Secretariat (CEC) and have the right to observe the census and 2021 voter registration in the provincial capital where it is on the list of national observers of that provincial capital. Second: Submission and receipt of applications for registration of international observers
The application deadline for registration as an international observer is from September 12, 2021 to February 9, 2022.
* International NGOs wishing to participate in the observation must collect the sample application form for registration of international observers at the NEC office and must submit their application for registration of observers at the NEC office during working hours. Morning from 7:00 to 11:30 and afternoon from 14:00 to 17:30 (Office of Political Parties and Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations, 9th floor).
International observers who have been officially recognized and authorized and have received an international observer ID card from the NEC have the right to observe the entire voter list and voter registration process in the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Third: Submission and receipt of applications for registration of political party agents
The deadline for submitting and receiving applications for registration of political party agents is from October 5, 2021 to February 2, 2022.
* A political party that is properly registered under the Law on Political Parties has the right to appoint some of its agents as required by the party to observe the examination of the voter list and voter registration at the commune / sangkat halls and at the commune voter registration offices. Sangkats determined by the NEC.
* Political parties must apply for registration of their agents to the Commune / Sangkat Council (CPC) on Form 1007, which is provided free of charge. A political party can make a copy of Form 1007 to use in applying for registration of its political party agent.
At the same time, the NEC hopes that the Association of National and International Non-Governmental Organizations, Political Parties, the CPP and the CEC will work together to promote the review of the voter list and voter registration. 2021 is a success.
Complaints about the hardships of raising chickens due to the Kovid 19 crisis and the sharp imports from Vietnam have been pouring out of the mouths of poultry farmers. Mr. Pol Vanda is a chicken farmer in Bor Seth district, Kampong Speu province.
Mr. Pol Vanda started his career as a chicken farmer since 2015, and raising chickens for the first time saw that it was profitable to support his livelihood and led him to expand his chicken farm continuously. According to Vanda, the profit at that time was due to the small number of domestic breeding and less imports from neighboring countries.
But with the arrival of Kovid 19, which narrowed the market, combined with the import of chickens from Vietnam, Pol Vanda reduced his chicken farm from 25,000 to 10,000. Two of his farms have also been closed.
Ms. Vun Eneang, 27 years old, is another chicken farmer in Khnach Khang Cheung village, Boeung Tranh Khang Cheung commune, Samrong district, Takeo province. She decided to switch from raising pigs to raising chickens in 2020 after her pigs contracted African swine fever and died and lost a lot of money.
Ms. Wun Ee Neang decided to invest tens of thousands of dollars in processing pigs into chicken coops, hoping that the chickens have a good market and can make a profit. But the results did not turn out as expected. The Kovid 19 crisis and the import of chickens from Vietnam cost her tens of thousands of dollars each time she caught a chicken.
In relation to this chicken issue, Mr. Srun Pov, President of the Cambodian Breeders' Association, claimed that Cambodian chicken farmers are able to raise enough to supply the market without importing. Mr. Srun Pov also said that at present, the Ministry of Agriculture does not have a policy to import more chickens from neighboring countries, but to import only about 1,000 chickens per day.
However, corrupt traders took the opportunity to make a profit at a time when Vietnamese chickens were in high demand due to Kovid 19 disease and the sharp fall in prices, which smuggled chickens into the Cambodian market. Especially at the border crossings in Tbong Khmum and Prey Veng provinces. According to Srun Pov, there are currently tens of thousands of chickens imported from Vietnam per day.
Mr. Pol Vanda and Ms. Vun Ee Neang expressed the same opinion that if they do not help prevent the import of chickens from neighboring countries during the 19 crisis of Kovid, their chicken raising will certainly collapse. Can not fight forward anymore.
In the context of the Kovid 19 outbreak, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, encouraged and encouraged farmers to work hard to raise animals and grow crops to support their livelihoods. The encouragement of the head of the Royal Government has pushed animal husbandry and cultivation seem to be gaining momentum. But the opening up of large numbers of animals from neighboring countries has left farmers severely frustrated with their efforts.
On the other hand, Cambodian farmers have invested a lot to invest in the construction of chicken raising infrastructure. Allowing the poultry industry to collapse due to the Kovid 19 crisis or the importation of chickens from neighboring countries without preventive measures is a major danger to the national economy as well as the supply of chickens when the situation in the country returns to normal. Livestock infrastructure is damaged and farmers may be tired of rearing.
Both the Animal Husbandry Association in Cambodia and the livestock farmers have joined together to urge the Royal Government and relevant ministries to help set out measures to address this challenge, especially to prevent the smuggling of chickens from Vietnam at the provincial ports. Tbong Khmum and Prey Veng in large numbers.
Mr. Srun Pov appealed, "I would like to call on all relevant institutions, especially border institutions, to help prevent it. It has a lot of effects, firstly affecting farmers, secondly affecting diseases, thirdly affecting consumers. At that point, I would like to call on the people at the border to help our farmers so that they can continue to live. Please help. ”
Mr. Pol Vanda insisted, "I, as a farmer, raise this request to all relevant authorities along the border and all relevant government officials at all levels, please help as much as possible not to import chickens. "Too much."
Mr. Srun Pov, President of Cambodian Breeders Association, Chicken Farmers, Chicken Farmers
