* Background of project initiation
In 2014, the European Union and the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, in collaboration with Africa and other regional partners, launched an action program against desertification to promote the Great Wall Green Initiative for the Sahara and the region. Sahel (GGWSSI).
In 2016, about 15 percent of the target area will be planted with trees, while 21 countries have plans to build a green wall, including the re-establishment of a green nature supported by farmers.
The Great Wall Green Initiative for the Sahara and Sahel (GGWSSI) aims to strengthen existing mechanisms (such as the Comprehensive African Agricultural Development Program, the Subregion Environment Program and the National Program to Combat Desert Expansion). ) To increase efficiency through integration and coordination activities.
The Regional Harmonization Strategy emphasizes stakeholder partnerships, integration into existing programs, sharing of lessons learned (especially through southern cooperation and technology transfer), regional engagement, and Ownership of activities and development of integrated plans and development of global plans.
The $ 8 billion project aims to rehabilitate 100 million hectares of damaged land by 2030, which will create 350,000 rural jobs and absorb 250 million tons of carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.
