WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden on Friday issued an executive order ordering his officials to review and release documents related to the FBI's September 11, 2001 terrorist attacks. This was released by the Xinhua News Agency on Saturday morning, September 4, 2021.
The order requires the US Department of Justice and other federal agencies to review the documents and release confidential information to the public within the next six months. "We must not forget the great suffering of the families and loved ones of the 2,977 innocent people who lost their lives in the terrorist attacks," Joe Biden said in a statement. "The worst in US history."
Many families of the victims of the "September 11" terrorist attacks have been trying for years to urge the US government to continue releasing more information about the event. September 11, 2021, which coincides with the next weekend, is the 20th anniversary of the "September 11" terrorist events.
