"20 years of the United States in Afghanistan is a tragedy," said Vladimir Putin.
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian President Vladimir Putin called 20 years of US presence in Afghanistan "tragic". The statement came after US President Joe Biden announced the end of the full military presence in Afghanistan on August 31 (Washington time). This is according to a release by Russian media Sputnik on Wednesday afternoon, September 1, 2021.
Putin also explained, "If we want to change something, it is impossible to try to change only the outer shell ... If someone wants to do something to connect with others, they should "Look at the history, culture and philosophy of life of these people in the broadest sense, as well as respect for their traditions."
In conclusion, Vladimir Putin described the 20 years of the United States in Afghanistan as a "tragedy" caused by the apathy and misunderstanding of the nature of the Afghan people. Putin stressed that if we do not understand the history, culture and philosophy, we will not be able to achieve "positive results".
