(Islamabad): Pakistani officials have expressed concern over security threats from neighboring Afghanistan as the Taliban try to form a new government and maintain stability in the country following the departure of US and NATO troops. . This was released by Reuters on Wednesday morning, September 1, 2021.
According to Pakistani officials, what they are worried about is that some separatist militants, such as the Taliban-Pakistan, will cross the border from Afghanistan and launch any attack on Pakistani territory.
"The next two to three months are a critical time ... Islamabad fears an escalation of attacks by militants," a Pakistani official told Reuters on condition of anonymity. "Fighting is along the border between Pakistan and Afghanistan as the Taliban try to form a new government after the fall of a Western-backed government."
Pakistan is believed to have been a strong supporter of the Taliban, as the United States has often accused Pakistan of providing shelter to Taliban leaders.
