Biden and Ghani chatted for about 14 minutes on July 23, 2021. On August 15, 2021, Ghani fled abroad and the Taliban entered the capital, Kabul. Tens of thousands of Afghans have since fled the country, and many more have been killed in suicide bombings at Kabul airport.
In the phone call, Biden offered to continue providing military assistance if Ghani could confirm his plans for the US withdrawal. Biden also instructed Ghani to seek the support of powerful groups in Afghanistan for a new military strategy and to appoint capable individuals to lead the effort.
Biden spent most of the conversation focusing on what he called the issue of the Afghan government's views at the time. The US president suggested Ghani should change his mind. However, neither Biden nor Ghani appeared to have commented on the possibility of the fall of the Afghan government, which took place just 23 days after the talks.
The United States completed the evacuation operation and the complete withdrawal of troops from Afghanistan on Monday, marking the official end of 20 years of military operations there.
