UN Secretary-General warns of worst humanitarian crisis in Afghanistan as 18 million people in need of food
Antonio Guterres said after the complete withdrawal of US troops from Afghanistan, "As Afghanistan enters a new phase, I am deeply concerned about the worsening humanitarian and economic crisis that will ensue in Afghanistan." "This country, as well as the threat of a complete collapse of basic services."
Today, almost half of Afghanistan's population, or 18 million people, need humanitarian aid to survive. One in three Afghans does not know where their next meal will come from. More than half of the total number of Afghan children under the age of five is expected to be severely malnourished by 2022. People are losing access to goods and services every day.
"Humanitarian catastrophe is happening," Antonio Guterres said. Now more and more Afghan children, women and men need the support and solidarity of the international community. The humanitarian system's commitment to accommodation and assistance will not be compromised. "By 2021, we need to provide assistance to 8 million Afghans."
