General knowledge: 5 countries that produce the most electricity in the world
(Phnom Penh): Electricity is a necessity for people's daily life. Many of these natural resources are being converted into electricity, such as coal, fossil fuels, solar oil, crude oil, as well as nuclear power.
The following are the top 5 countries that produce the most electricity in the world:
5th place: Japan
Japan has traditionally relied heavily on nuclear power to generate electricity due to its limited crude oil and natural gas resources. Currently, more than a third of Japan's electricity is generated from fossil fuels, but the use of hydropower and solar panels is also on the rise in the island nation.
Russia ranks fourth with a maximum capacity of 1,085 kilowatt hours by 2020, accounting for 4.1 percent of the world's total electricity generation. Russia has a maximum capacity of more than 220 gigawatts of electricity from nearly 440 power plants.
Most of Russia's electricity generation comes from gas and coal-fired power plants, with renewable energy sources accounting for about 17 percent by 2020. In terms of nuclear power, Russia has 31 reactors operating with a capacity of 21 gigawatts.
The United States is the world's second largest producer of electricity by 2020 at 4,286 kilowatt hours. Thirty-five percent of that comes from natural gas and other fuels, while 23 percent comes from coal and nearly 20 percent from nuclear power.
