In the first six months of this year, more than 2,000 Cambodians have been rescued from human trafficking, fraud, illegal cross-border brokers and unbearable working conditions.
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Chou Bun Eng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Permanent Vice President of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking (NEC), said that in the first six months of 2021, Cambodians who suffered from trafficking cases 2,317 people who cheated, illegally crossed the border and could not stand the working conditions were rescued by the Cambodian official office abroad and sent back to their homeland.
A senior official of the Ministry of Interior said in an interview with Fresh News regarding the progress of the Royal Government of Cambodia's efforts to combat human trafficking.
According to Ms. Chou Bun Eng, in 2020, 1,574 Cambodian victims of illegal work were rescued and repatriated by the Cambodian official abroad.
According to the official, in relation to the work of cracking down on human trafficking and exploitation in the first six months of 2021, the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking had 198 cases, an increase of 135 cases compared to the same period in 2020, which was only 63. Case.
Ms. Chou Bun Eng said that in cracking down on all 198 cases, the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking arrested 291 suspects and rescued 721 victims, including 139 cases of labor trafficking that arrested people. 231 suspects and 59 cases of sexual exploitation, which arrested 60 suspects.
She noted that of the 139 cases of human trafficking, most of them were also illegal cross-border exports, with 121 arrests and 198 arrests. There are many other types of human trafficking and exploitation, but the ones raised are big and common.
For many such crackdowns, Ms. Chou Bun Eng explained that from the relevant partners cooperate well. The Royal Government of Cambodia, under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, is committed to cooperating with all relevant partners at the national and regional levels to eliminate all forms of human trafficking and exploitation in order to promote respect for human rights, dignity and social justice.
