30,000 families in 5 provinces receive food from the World Food Program in collaboration with the Ministry of Education
(Phnom Penh): Throughout the week, the World Food Program (WFP) has partnered with the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports to distribute food to 30,000 vulnerable families, including a total of about 150,000 people. People in five provinces: Siem Reap, Oddar Meanchey, Kampong Thom, Kampong Chhnang and Pursat.
This distribution will take place every day from August 30 to September 3, 2021. This is according to a press release from the World Food Program, which was received by Fresh News on August 31.
This assistance is provided to families identified by authorities as vulnerable, have poor family identification (IDPoor) and have children enrolled in schools that are supported by the World Food Program and the Ministry of Food and Drug Administration. Youth education and sports.
This is the fifth round of food distribution that comes as the Royal Government of Cambodia reaffirms its commitment to the State Alliance to implement a school-based food program, which opens as part of the Global Food System Summit in Phnom Penh. September 2021.
The coalition includes governments, the United Nations, civil society, educational institutions, humanitarian organizations, and the private sector, recognizing that school meals are vital to changing food systems and can help individual countries recover from the economic crisis. - The society caused by Kovid-19 disease and achieve the goal of sustainable development.
Cambodia was one of the first 40 countries to sign up to this historic alliance, which could help protect the most vulnerable children from starvation and malnutrition.
For 20 years, the World Food Program (WFP) School Feeding Program has provided nutritious food to primary school children in some of Cambodia's poorest areas.
Prior to Kovid-19, the program provided healthy and nutritious food to 223,000 children in 908 target schools in five provinces. Since the outbreak of Kovid-19 and school closures, students no longer have a nutritious breakfast. This has particularly affected children from the poorest families, many of whom have suffered from loss of income and employment.
"The World Food Program (WFP) recognizes that good nutrition is essential for the development of children, and that is why our team is working to ensure that both packages "This reaches out to vulnerable families to ensure that children have access to food during the Kovid crisis."
