Senior official of the Ministry of Interior: In the first 6 months of this year, Cambodia cracked down on 198 cases of human trafficking and exploitation, an increase of 135 cases compared to last year
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Chou Bun Eng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Permanent Vice President of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking (NEC), said that in the first six months of 2021, Cambodia will crack down on human trafficking and exploitation. 198 cases.
A senior official of the Ministry of Interior confirmed on August 27, 2021 that the number of cases of human trafficking and exploitation in 198 cases increased by 135 cases compared to the same period in 2020, which was only 63 cases.
In all crackdowns, the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking arrested 291 suspects and rescued 721 victims, she said.
Ms. Chou Bun Eng added that in the crackdown on human trafficking and exploitation in 198 cases, there were 139 cases of human trafficking in labor, which arrested 231 suspects, and 59 cases of sex trafficking in arresting suspects. 60 people.
She noted that of the 139 cases of human trafficking, most of them were illegal cross-border shipments, with 121 arresting 198 suspects. There are many more types of human trafficking and exploitation, but these are big and common cases.
When asked why there will be less crime in 2020? Ms. Chou Bun Eng stated that because 2020 is the time when Kovid 19 disease first strikes, everyone is still worried, while Cambodia also has measures to close the border, the perpetrators have not found a way.
"But at the beginning of 2021, there was a resurgence of activity related to this activity. "But we are not indifferent. We have cracked down on 198 cases, arrested 291 suspects and rescued 721 victims." This was confirmed by a senior official of the Ministry of Interior.
Speaking on behalf of Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, Ms. Chou Bun Eng also stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia is committed to cooperating with all relevant partners at the national and regional levels. Eliminate all forms of human trafficking and exploitation to promote respect for human rights, dignity and social justice.
The South Korean government, through KOICA, provides education to Cambodian officials every year. Cambodian officials often go directly to South Korea for this training. But in 2021, due to the epidemic situation, 19 KOICA has turned to online training instead.
The purpose of the training is to "strengthen the social welfare policy" to strengthen the social welfare policy capacity of Cambodian officials in supporting poverty reduction and improving the quality of life of persons with disabilities, women and children.
This year, KOICA, in collaboration with the Institute for Human Resource Development for Health and Wellness of the Republic of Korea, launched an online training course on "Strengthening Social Welfare Policy" from August 30 to September 7, 2021.
This important training course is designed to: First: Share experiences on social welfare in Korea through online lectures and workshops, and Second: Review and revise the action plan developed during the year course. First, in 2020, through an analysis of social welfare policies in Cambodia.
In addition, participants will develop an action plan led by a Korean professor. This plan will be an important document for the participants in this training program to find ways as well as solutions to the issues raised in the country report.
