The Cambodian Embassy states that the use of the words and logos "Phnom Penh Noodle Products" and "Angkor Wat logo" without the permission of the competent authorities of Cambodia violates the provisions of Cambodian law, agreements and international treaties, in particular The use of the Phnom Penh Noodle brand has left the general consumer confused about the origin of packaged noodle products.
"The embassy has researched, targeted and assigned commercial attachments to the embassy to visit the companies that import those products to the company's location and has issued an official notification to the companies to stop importing or placing them," the statement said. "We will continue to sell such products and the embassy will take further action in case the company refuses to comply with the notice."
Seang Thai, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, confirmed that the Ministry of Commerce needs to continue researching the origin of the dried noodles to determine which country it is made in, while presumably in Vietnam. If the source of the manufacturer is known, the Ministry will take action to stop it immediately.
He added that the problem was rampant and that the Ministry of Commerce, in collaboration with the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation and the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, had taken action by sending letters to foreign embassies asking them to immediately stop the companies. Use the image of Angkor Wat or our Cambodian flag.
