International Analysis: What is the US Strategy in the Indo-Pacific?
(Singapore): During a keynote foreign policy speech during a visit to Singapore on August 24, US Vice President Kamala Harris directly criticized China for intimidating neighboring countries. The parties to the dispute in the South China Sea. But China has denied the allegations and accused the United States of breaking ties between China and other countries in the region.
The call was interpreted by some experts as "because Donald Trump wants to put in place a new policy that does not involve the old policy of former US President Barack Obama, who spent his entire second term in office." In pushing US foreign policy away from the Middle East to focus on the emerging economic zone, "Asia."
The continued use of the term "Indo-Pacific" assumes that the United States has become increasingly focused on China, and that this may be reflected in the restoration of the quadratic strategic alliance, also known as Quad, which includes members such as the United States, Japan, and Australia. And India. Former US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo has stressed that the international community needs to form a "new democratic alliance" to counter China.
Therefore, for the sake of economic interests with China, most countries are trying to use a balance-based strategy based on diplomacy, not on the side of any superpower, and even though some countries are in territorial disputes with China, They do not dare to upset or anger China when it comes to economic interests.
Kamala Harris has acknowledged that the United States will host the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Forum in 2023, emphasizing the importance of the Southeast Asian market to the United States. She added that ASEAN countries are the fourth largest export partner of the United States and that this trade relationship employs more than 600,000 Americans.
