Spokesperson: The expression of concern of a small number of civil society groups on the establishment of Cambodian human rights institutions is political (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Chin Malin, Spokesman of the Cambodian Human Rights Committee, stated that Expressing concern about the establishment of the Cambodian National Human Rights Institution by these 60 civil society groups has only shown its political character and irresponsibility.
The response by senior Cambodian Human Rights Committee officials came after these civil society groups expressed concern over the establishment of a national human rights body and accused it of being merely an excuse to prove that the government was considering a solution. Human rights and the human rights situation in Cambodia is deteriorating.
Mr. Chin Malin told Fresh News this morning, August 25, 2021, "These 60 civil society groups' expressions of concern about the establishment of the Cambodian National Human Rights Institution reflect the political character and There is no responsibility of this handful of civil society groups because:
First: "From the beginning, civil society groups have been proposing to establish this institution and have been organizing it themselves since 1997. As this work has been in the hands of civil society for too long and has not materialized, the Royal Government has decided to do it again, assigning this task to the Cambodian Human Rights Committee to lead and organize in 2019.
Second, this institution has not yet taken shape, just in the beginning stage, they are already evaluating, blocking and criticizing the effectiveness of this institution. They should not worry too much about the unborn child, but instead should be open-minded, cooperative and support the establishment of this institution to be successful and meet the real needs of all parties. Relevant is more useful for the society.
Third, the first draft of this law was submitted to these civil society groups through the Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR) for review and comment, but they did not borrow and comment. Instead, criticize and prevent the establishment of this institution.
Fourth, expressing their concern about the Paris Principles and other international principles concerning the independence, participation and mandate of this institution made it clear that they did not actually look at and read the first draft of this law. No, because these principles are already included in the first draft of the law.
And 5: For the human rights perspective of a handful of civil society groups, it is only when the party they support and their allies come to power that Cambodia respects human rights and democracy and is the right time to establish This institution, which clearly shows their agenda and political activities by taking refuge under the banner of civil society organizations in Cambodia.
