(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Cambodian Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said that Cambodia does not need a printed democracy or a Western-style democracy.
Dr. Kin Phea made this remark during a roundtable discussion on "Cambodia between China and the United States" organized by the Royal Academy of Cambodia on August 19, 2021.
Dr. Kin Phea said: "Cambodia does not need a printed democracy, Cambodia is not a Western democracy, you are afraid it can not grow, it can not grow in the Cambodian context, as we know that democracy grows. "If possible, it should reflect the historical, social and cultural context of the country where the democracy was adopted."
According to Dr. Kin Phea, democracy for Cambodia requires a change of leadership and a transfer of power, so what we want to say is that a change of leadership must come through elections, not a different image to come to power in all of Cambodia. Apart from the election.
At this point, Mr. Kin Phea explained, "The principle of law-abiding practice is not only from the top leadership, but also from the top to the bottom, and especially the leadership must show exemplary, accurate and impartial law enforcement." "There are no exceptions, the law is not standardized."
Dr. Kin Phea added that whether the opposition, politicians, activists, civil society organizations or ordinary people and the law enforcement authorities are accused of injustice and lack of freedom, so this statement is not correct. If we want the rule of law, we must all obey the law.
Dr. Kin Phea said that apart from the political space, the countries around Cambodia, no country has as many political parties as Cambodia. This point clearly shows the political space that the Royal Government has paved the way for the people to have sufficient rights to do politics.
