She was originally from Mondulkiri (Sen Monorom City) and served as a soldier during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era. . When the Khmer Rouge came to power, her family was torn apart by her husband and her five children, who were beaten to death by the Khmer Rouge, leaving her alone and paralyzed. .
Later, she lived alone until after the liberation on January 7, 1979, when she came to live near the Chroy Changva bridge in Phnom Penh and made a living, but she could not live alone and was disabled. Not comfortable, but she still struggles with everything.
Due to old age, the business of selling shellfish is less regular, so the income is also irregular. During this period, Cambodia is also suffering from the crisis of the spread of Kovid-19 disease, making it even more difficult for her.
She said that today the force is getting weaker and weaker, however, if you do not work hard, there is no money to pay rent and no food.
