Banteay Srei Forestry Administration denies Virak Buntham allegations of extortion in exchange for vehicle release
(Siem Reap): On August 17, the Banteay Srei District Forestry Administration seized 984 geckos from an illegal timber trader in a Virak Buntham truck. The car company claimed that authorities had extorted $ 30,000 in exchange for his release.
Mr. Mong Bunlim added that the truck carrying illegal geckos was transported from Preah Vihear province. Arriving in Siem Reap, he was stopped by the Banteay Srei District Forestry Administration. He said that transporting geckos is illegal because it is a mammal, such as lizards, which is in violation of Article 96, point 19, to be fined.
Mr. Mong Bunlim added that for the puppets that the Banteay Srei District Forestry Administration caught according to the forest law, all specimens or objects that could not be stored were burned or buried. Currently stored at Banteay Srei District Forestry Administration.
Mr. Bunlim also denied the company's allegations that his forestry administration had extorted $ 30,000 from the company in exchange for agreeing to release the vehicle. "Unlike the allegations, my officials just said that the illegal goods, if weighed in kilograms, is equivalent to a fine of about 30,000 dollars," he said.
It should also be noted that this is not the first case that the Banteay Srei District Forestry Administration has cracked down on the pup trade. A few years ago, the Forestry Administration had already cracked down on two cases of illegally transporting geckos, some carrying live geckos and some being roasted.
