He said that currently the Royal Government of Cambodia has about 220,000 civil servants. This number does not include the armed forces. Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) resumes Case 002/02 appeal against Khieu Samphan for a third day
(Phnom Penh): The Supreme Court Chamber of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) resumes its hearing on 18 August 2021 for the third day of the appeal hearing in Case 002/02 against Khieu Samphan. Former President of Democratic Kampuchea.
This third day of the Supreme Court Chamber hearings focused on topics including "Arguments of Appeals Concerning Khieu Samphan's Personal Criminal Liability" and "Arguments of Relevant Appeals "To the verdict."
Khieu Samphan's defense argues that the Trial Chamber's decision does not contain clear evidence to place charges against its client, such as co-crimes and other criminal liability. On the accused Khieu Samphan.
Khieu Samphan's lawyer, who has been speaking since the 16 August hearing, asked the Supreme Court Chamber to consider and annul the verdict of the Trial Chamber, which sentenced Khieu Samphan to life imprisonment. Including the annulment of genocide charges against Vietnamese nationals convicted of Khieu Samphan, citing weak evidence.
However, this argument was dismissed by the Co-Prosecutors and the Co-Lawyers, the Prime Minister's co-lawyers, citing a number of powers, including the fairness of the proceedings that the Trial Chamber carried out in the trial, including its jurisdiction. Raised and charged, along with the facts charged against Khieu Samphan.
Khieu Samphan, former President of the State Council of Democratic Kampuchea, has filed an appeal with the Supreme Court Chamber against the ECCC Trial Chamber's verdict found guilty. Genocide, crimes against humanity and war crimes were sentenced to life in prison on November 16, 2018.
The Trial Chamber found that Khieu Samphan, the head of state of the Democratic Kampuchea regime, had joined the joint venture with other senior leaders of the Communist Party of Kampuchea with the aim of carrying out a speedy socialist revolution through crimes.
The Office of the Co-Prosecutors did not appeal the conviction, but appealed in connection with the forced marriage, requesting the Supreme Court Chamber to conclude that the Trial Chamber was at fault when the Trial Chamber Conclude that men who are forced to have sex at their marriages are not victims of crimes against humanity of other inhumane acts.
The Co-Prosecutors request that the finding of guilt be left aside and that convictions for other inhumane crimes be corrected, including sexual violence against male victims, so that both male and female victims Of the same coercive acts are properly recognized. This is in accordance with Rule 110 (4) of the Internal Rules, as Khieu Samphan has already been convicted of other inhumane crimes.
