Former US president who launched war on terror in Afghanistan urges government to expedite aid to Afghan refugees
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - Former United States President George W. Bush, who launched the "war on terror" in Afghanistan and elsewhere after the September 11, 2001 attacks, has urged the US government to step up its efforts to provide immediate assistance to Afghan refugees. After the Taliban quickly seized power in recent days. This is according to the release by Reuters on Tuesday night, August 17, 2021.
Bush and former First Lady Laura Bush said in a statement Monday evening that the US government has the legitimate authority to welcome refugees in the wake of the devastating humanitarian crisis. "We have the responsibility and resources to ensure a safe passage for them as soon as possible without delaying bureaucracy."
The Bush family said they believed the evacuation efforts would be effective with the help of US forces, adding that a strong US ally, along with private NGOs, was ready to help. .
Under former US President George W. Bush, US forces overthrew the Taliban government, which organized al Qaeda militants responsible for the 2001 airstrikes in New York and Washington.
