WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Joe Biden said in July that the Afghan government would not collapse because of the large number of Afghan troops and better weapons than the Taliban. Biden made the remarks in response to a reporter who asked him if a return to the Taliban was inevitable.
A month later, the Afghan army collapsed and lost almost all of its territory without fighting the Taliban. The Kabul government collapsed so quickly. The Taliban entered the capital, Kabul, on August 15 and is now preparing to return to power in Afghanistan.
The United States and NATO have spent billions of dollars, with the United States alone spending $ 83 billion over the past 20 years to help train the Afghan army. But why is Afghanistan still collapsing? Why is the Afghan military not fighting to stop the Taliban?
* Army discouraged
Of course, only Afghan special forces fought the Taliban to the end, while most of the army was willing to surrender to save their lives rather than die for a hopeless cause.
The rapid collapse of the Afghan government has raised questions about the US military's mistakes in training Afghan troops for more than 20 years. US military training has seen a number of shortcomings, ranging from a lack of linguistic and cultural knowledge, with a focus on the military, with little focus on police training, which has undermined regional security.
