Lim Chea Vutha Question 1:
As the world knows, China was the first country to be infected with Kovid 19 in Wuhan, but more importantly, China was able to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 in its country. In this regard, I would like to express the strength and measures of the government that have been put in place and have been successful in sharing with other countries that are suffering from this deadly virus.
Chinese Ambassador First Answer:
It has three main aspects:
Third, the participation of the Chinese people throughout the country. Immediately after the COVID-19 erupted, 1.4 billion Chinese people showed great solidarity in overcoming challenges. From the capital to the countryside, people from all walks of life have come together shoulder to shoulder to show the ingenuity and strength of the public force that keeps the virus at bay.
Lim Chea Vutha Question 2:
Chinese Ambassador Answer 2:
At the same time, the effectiveness and safety of Chinese vaccines have been fully demonstrated in Cambodia. Yuk Sambath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Health of Cambodia, on August 12, 2021, on the occasion of receiving the new Chinese vaccine, said that although the Delta virus has spread rapidly, the number of new cases in Cambodia has not jumped significantly. Clearly, Chinese vaccines are highly effective against the new mutation virus.
Lim Chea Vutha Question 3:
Chinese Ambassador Answer 3:
However, the United States has turned the global epidemic crisis into a political one and has tried to discriminate against China by using terms such as "Wuhan virus" and using surveillance as a weapon to attack. China to shift responsibility for its failure to respond to the COVID-19 crisis and to achieve its political goals is to discredit and block other countries.
Politicians, the media, experts and people from many countries have come forward to accuse the United States of trying to turn viral work into politics. More than 20 million Chinese people have also submitted online petitions to the WHO urging an investigation into the Fort Detrick Biological Laboratory in the United States.
I would like to emphasize that turning viral surveillance into a political issue is the biggest obstacle to COVID-19 crisis efforts, as China's position on global viral surveillance remains unchanged and presented. Most clearly.
First, the study of the origin of viruses is a scientific task. It should and can only be left to scientists to conduct research, through scientific research and finding sources of the evolution of viruses and animal-to-human transmission. No country has the right to take its own political advantage over human life or take this scientific issue to fulfill its own ambitions and attack other countries.
Second, the findings and opinions expressed in the joint report of the China-WHO working group are widely recognized by the international community and scientists, and everyone must respect and implement all aspects, including the WHO. . Future work to trace the source of the virus globally should and must be done on an existing basis, rather than re-establishing a new direction.
Viruses never know the boundaries and do not distinguish between races. China is just as affected by the global pandemic as any other country. Given the ongoing epidemic and mutation of the virus, the most urgent need now is to jointly increase the supply of vaccines against COVID-19 and promote solidarity and cooperation.
The search for the origin of the virus must be based on scientific principles, and its involvement in politics must be vehemently denied. China will continue to work with stakeholders to study the global origin of the virus on a scientific basis and will donate what China has to help make the world a complete success on the COVID-19 pandemic.
Lim Chea Vutha Question 4:
Apart from China itself, which produces 19 Kovid vaccines for its people and to provide assistance to many countries around the world, do Chinese friends plan to establish new facilities for vaccine production outside of China? For example, Cambodia. At the same time, while Cambodia's demand for vaccines is still in demand for its people, does China's ally plan to provide more vaccines to Cambodia?
Chinese Ambassador Answer 4:
Since the outbreak, China has donated 800 million doses of vaccine to countries around the world, including Cambodia, in fulfillment of President Xi Jinping's strong commitment to vaccinating. China provides universal public benefits.
China has already provided 21.7 million doses of COVID-19 vaccine to Cambodia, of which 3.2 million doses is in support of the Cambodian government's plan to vaccinate 10 million people. Across the country. China will continue to provide vaccines and other medical equipment to Cambodia in its capacity to act as a strategic backbone for Cambodia.
