(Phnom Penh): His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, has issued a Royal Decree appointing Mr. Ben Rath as Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction. This is according to the Royal Decree obtained by Fresh News on August 17.
On August 10, 2020, Mr. Ben Rath was appointed by the King by Royal Decree to be an advisor to the Ministry of Interior with the rank of Undersecretary of State after his retirement from the National Police.
Mr. Ben Rath, a former Deputy Commissioner of Phnom Penh Municipal Police, was previously appointed as Kampong Cham Provincial Police Commissioner and Tbong Khmum Provincial Police Commissioner before retiring in 2020.
Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (ECCC) continues to hear Case 002/02 appeal against Khieu Samphan
Defendants for the Accused, Office of the Co-Prosecutors and the Lead Co-Lawyers of the Civil Parties presented their arguments before the Supreme Court Chamber.
Defendant Khieu Samphan's defense argued its appeal, trying to prove that the Trial Chamber had procedural and jurisdictional errors in the charges against its client.
The Office of the Co-Prosecutors did not appeal the conviction, but appealed in connection with the forced marriage, requesting the Supreme Court Chamber to conclude that the Trial Chamber was at fault when the Trial Chamber Conclude that men who are forced to have sex at their marriages are not victims of crimes against humanity of other inhumane acts.
The Civil Party Lead Co-Lawyers respectfully request the Supreme Court Chamber to: Firstly dismiss Khieu Samphan's appeal, secondly, to uphold all Khieu Samphan's guilt and thirdly Khieu Samphan sentenced to life in prison
Recall that Khieu Samphan, former President of the State Council of Democratic Kampuchea, has lodged a complaint with the Supreme Court Chamber against the judgment of the ECCC Trial Chamber. Convicted of genocide, crimes against humanity and war crimes, he was sentenced to life in prison on November 16, 2018.
Khieu Samphan was also convicted of aiding and abetting crimes against humanity through homicides at cooperatives and security departments for deaths caused by living conditions at the site. These crimes include shortages of food, water and health care, as well as restrictions on hard work.
The Trial Chamber found that Khieu Samphan had incited and legitimized criminal policies and had made significant contributions to crimes committed by Communist Party of Kampuchea cadres. He directly instructed the cadres on the implementation of criminal policy and was responsible for training the Communist Party of Cambodia cadres.
In addition, the Trial Chamber found that Khieu Samphan had been involved in the purge of enemies throughout the country and had approved "the right to destroy" in the lower ranks of the Communist Party of Kampuchea. Khieu Samphan was also responsible for delivering a speech in support of the widely publicized Communist Party of Cambodia policy, which the Chamber found to be a contribution to the commission of crime.
The Trial Chamber found that Khieu Samphan was responsible for the policies of the Communist Party of Kampuchea targeting Vietnamese, Cham, Buddhist and former Khmer Republic governors and their families. The Chamber found that between 1975 and 1976 there was a nationwide policy to deport Vietnamese living in Cambodia.
The Cham people were forced to eat pork and were forbidden to worship and speak their native language. In addition, the Chamber found that large numbers of Cham civilians had been arrested and killed.
Officials of the Khmer Republic were also targeted for arrest and execution with their families. The Trial Chamber found that Khieu Samphan was responsible for a nationwide policy of identifying individuals who had been targeted. Forced arrangements often involve marrying people they do not know.
