After overthrowing the old Taliban regime and establishing a new government as its backbone, the United States has not helped improve the situation in Afghanistan. The United States has finally decided to withdraw, allowing the Taliban to retake the country and bring back the misery of the entire Afghan people.
The current war in Afghanistan also made this Cambodian historian see the similarities to the war in Cambodia from 1970 to 1975. In particular, the fall of the Khmer Republic of Lon Nol on April 17, 1975. Both governments collapsed after the United States overthrew a regime that was loyal to the United States.
"If we look at the current situation in Afghanistan, the so-called divisions, the so-called attacks, the wars, the chaos, the panic everywhere is no different from Cambodia. The same in Cambodia, during the Khmer Republic in 1975, when Cambodia was abandoned by the United States. This is the confirmation of Mr. Ros Chantraboth.
Ros Chantraboth added: "So we can imagine the future seeing that those who used to cooperate with the US military with American politicians will have a lot of problems in the future. There could be killings and genocides like in Cambodia. "So now we are waiting to see if the Afghan politicians and leaders will flee the country like the Khmer politicians in the Lon Nol era, the Khmer Republic."
The war in Afghanistan also reminded Professor Heng Vanda, an observer of Cambodian social events and the leader of a resistance movement to overthrow the Sangkum regime, remembering the events of the fall of the Khmer Republic. Such as the event of the evacuation of Cambodian diplomats and government officials from Phnom Penh on April 12, 1975.
Mr. Heng Vanda said, "People at that time were all soft and could not think. To deceive the Americans, I miss being angry with the Americans, so I no longer trust the Americans. "Our story with the Khmer Rouge is a bloody story, and the tragedy is almost dead, willing to die."
Mr. Heng Vanda added, "The story is very bloody, it is cheating the United States. At that time, they led themselves to make a revolution, to overthrow the revolutionary king, and to declare a republic with the students protesting in front of the palace, wanting a new American regime, lest they get a lot of money. "Let it be safe, where the United States will run away."
Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, also saw that the current war in Afghanistan is no different from the war in Cambodia from 1970 to 1975. In particular, the tragic aftermath of the fall of the Afghan and Khmer Rouge regimes after the US withdrawal.
The United States' move to overthrow the current Afghan government, as well as the overthrow of the Khmer Republic in 1975, has led Dr. Kin Phea to view the United States as a "selfish" country and then abandon it. Allies without any emotion at all.
With the outbreak of the war in Afghanistan, Dr. Kin Phea also asked Cambodian politicians, especially Americans, to remember the American word, counting only the useful times, when the useless ones give up.
The US-backed Afghan government is on the verge of collapse after the United States decided to withdraw its troops, which it has ruled for about 20 years. The Taliban have seized control of several cities and are close to capturing Kabul.
In his statement, Biden said he had agreed to increase the deployment of nearly 5,000 troops to Kabul to facilitate the evacuation of the remaining US embassy and civilian personnel, as well as civilians. Some Afghans have served in the United States.
In addition, Biden also warned the Taliban that it would have to pay a fair price if it dared to attack or endanger US troops. According to Joe Biden, US troops will be completely withdrawn from Afghanistan before August 31.
Such a hasty withdrawal without a peace deal for Afghanistan is being criticized by Biden, but the US president has defended his decision, insisting that even if US troops remain in Afghanistan, one or five For years to come, nothing can change if the Afghan military is incompetent or unable to control their own country.
