Captures 65 frozen pork and mice imported from Thailand
(Kampong Chhnang): 65 frozen pigs weighing a total of 420 kg illegally transported from Thailand to Phnom Penh were intercepted by the General Department of Animal Health in collaboration with officials of the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Veterinary Production Office in the early hours of August 15. 2021, along National Road 5, O Rong village, Chhouk Sor commune, Kampong Tralach district.
An official from the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Department of Agriculture's Production and Veterinary Office said that the force of the Provincial Veterinary Production Office confiscated a white and red Hyundai truck, a two-and-a-half ton truck with the license plate PSHN0319, transporting frozen pigs. Three large barrels of 65 pigs weighing 420 kilograms and 30 kilograms of pig offal were shipped from Thailand.
Officials said the frozen pigs were smuggled into Cambodia without a license. The frozen pork was stored at the animal sanctuary of the Production and Veterinary Office of the Provincial Department of Agriculture in Kampong Chhnang to take samples to check and build a case according to legal procedures. More than 1.5 tons of illegally imported pork meat and offal was cracked down on
(Banteay Meanchey): 1,500 kilograms of pork meat and offal were seized by officers from the Production and Veterinary Office of the Banteay Meanchey Provincial Department of Agriculture in collaboration with the Malai District Military Forces on August 14 on a nearby road. Boundary line in Santepheap village, Toul Pong commune, Malai district.
Experts said that the pork meat and offal was transported in a Korean car with license plate number Battambang 3A-2561. There are 30 cases of pork meat and offal, each case weighs 50 kg.
After the arrest, no one came forward to take responsibility for the goods. The owner of the truck carrying the meat and internal organs confessed that he was hired to transport the meat, did not know the owner of the goods, and the meat was planned to be transported to Battambang province.
Huy Touch, director of the Production and Veterinary Office of the Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that the meat did not have a permit from the Ministry and that the packaging was not in accordance with the standards or technical standards. If the delivery is standard or technical standards but not legal, you will only be fined.
He said that the meat and offal of the pigs had reported to the prosecutor of the provincial court to ask for the principle of burning. Mr. Huy Touch pointed out that all these animals can be imported from Thailand through the corridor.
