A study has found that many white-tailed deer in the United States are infected with the Kovid 19 virus.
BEIJING (Reuters) - In a recent study published in the "Nature" magazine in the United Kingdom, the analysis and research of 19 anti-Kovid virus antibodies on serum samples of some white-tailed deer in the Northeast Researchers at the US Department of Agriculture have found that one-third of the white-tailed deer sampled contained 19 anti-Kovid antibodies and that they had been infected with the Kovid 19 virus.
Researchers released a report on the study online on July 29. However, this research report has not yet been evaluated and discussed by other researchers.
According to the report, the researchers found that of the white-tailed deer serum samples collected in early 2020, three tested positive for antibodies to the virus. The serum samples collected in 2019 contained one serum sample that detected 19 antibodies to Kovid virus.
