Taiwan, which China considers its only province under international law, in July announced the establishment of a representative office in Lithuania, Vilnius, named after Taiwan. The announcement was called by Beijing a diplomatic insult and that the decision to allow Taiwan to set up a representative office in Lithuania was a serious blow to China's sovereignty and territorial integrity.
In response, the Chinese Foreign Ministry told the Lithuanian government to recall its ambassador to China, and shortly thereafter, China also recalled its ambassador to Lithuania.
The Chinese Foreign Ministry issued a clear warning message: "The Chinese government and people are constantly committed to achieving national reunification. "The red line of defense of national sovereignty and territorial integrity is not to be missed."
"China urges the Lithuanian side to rectify its wrongdoing immediately by taking concrete steps to repair the damage to relations and not continue to go the wrong way," the Chinese Foreign Ministry said. "We also warn the Taiwanese authorities that the independence of Taiwan is a dead end and that any attempt to secede on the international stage will fail."
Relations between China and Lithuania have been strained since May, after the Baltic states announced their withdrawal from the China-17-plus-1 cooperation forum. Lithuania also promised to donate 20,000 doses of COVID-19 vaccine to Taiwan.
Some workers at the border after the Royal Government closed the border, Major General Seng Thearin asked for the understanding of the people on this difficulty
(Banteay Meanchey): Despite the Royal Government of Cambodia's decision to close the border between Cambodia and Thailand, some Cambodian workers continue to leave their homes in Thailand to return to Cambodia. The forced departure from Thailand to Cambodia has prevented some border workers from entering the country.
The Royal Government of Cambodia on July 28 decided to close the border between Cambodia and Thailand for two weeks, not allowing transit, except for the transport of goods and special cases. The decision was made to prevent the spread of Kovid 19, especially the new "Delta-Delta" virus, which is transmitted through workers returning from Thailand.
Despite this decision, some workers still leave their homes in Thailand in order to return to Cambodia. Due to the closure of the border, some workers have been stranded on the Thai side of the border.
On August 10, 2021, Major General Seng Thearin, Deputy Commander of the 5th Army Division, visited Cambodian workers resting on the border.
The lieutenant general received understanding and tolerance from the Cambodian workers who were temporarily staying near the border.
He said that the border closure measures that the Royal Government has put in place to prevent the spread of Kovid 19, especially the new "Data" virus into the community, will cause serious disaster in the community.
The lieutenant general also confirmed that the Royal Government as well as the forces and local authorities have worked together to consider and not leave the workers staying at the border alone. Lt. Gen. Seng Thearin instructed workers along the border that in case of health problems or other problems to report to the nearest forces so that the forces report to the authorities to help solve.
