Thai PM finally decides to "withdraw order" over media restrictions during Kovid-19 outbreak
The decision was made on Monday, but the Royal Gazette was only published on Tuesday. The order of Mr. Prayuth Chan Ocha was issued on July 29, 2021 to strictly prohibit the dissemination of information that may cause fear during the outbreak of COVID-19 because it will affect the order National security and peace.
Under the rules, Thai government authorities have the right to monitor all media outlets for violators, and they have the right to order Internet service providers to block the broadcast of such content. Cooperate with the National Police.
However, immediately after the announcement of the order, criticism from the public and especially from the media and human rights defenders in Thailand erupted and a petition was submitted to the court. To demand that the government of Mr. Prayuth withdraw the above order.
In the end, the Thai court ruled that the Thai government's order deprived the people of their rights and freedoms without the need to do so, and that having the power to cut off internet service was against the law because the internet was a means to an end. The most important thing for people to know about COVID-19 during the epidemic. Therefore, the court decided to withdraw the order.
